diff --git a/crates/engine/tests/smallm_gemv_bit_exact.rs b/crates/engine/tests/smallm_gemv_bit_exact.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7404ecb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/crates/engine/tests/smallm_gemv_bit_exact.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,895 @@
+//! Four-row verify GEMV vs the shipped M=1 decode kernel, in **both** packed
+//! layouts.
+//!
+//! # The two kernels, and the layout each belongs to
+//!
+//! | `perm_mode` | packed layout | four-row kernel | M=1 reference |
+//! |---|---|---|---|
+//! | 1 | column-major | `linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_colmajor_2col_sk2_m4` | `linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_colmajor_2col` |
+//! | 0 | row-major | `linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_rm_m4` | `linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_w2` |
+//!
+//! The family is layout-specific and the two are **not** interchangeable.
+//! `_colmajor_2col_sk2_m4` decodes the column-major ring; `_rm_m4` decodes the
+//! row-major one (k outermost: tile `(tk, tn)` lives at `(tk * tiles_n + tn) *
+//! WORDS`, which is why both kernels step `tk` by `stride = tiles_n * WORDS`).
+//! Pointing either at the wrong layout does not fault -- it decodes the wrong
+//! weights and the model emits noise.
+//!
+//! # Why this file exists in this shape
+//!
+//! That mis-target is not hypothetical; it is how this file came to exist. The
+//! shipped checkpoint is **row-major** (`KAPLUMBA_SMALLM_LOG=1` reports `perm=0`
+//! for all 411 tensors, and `KAPLUMBA_TRACE_GEMV=1` shows the M=1 decode kernel
+//! is `linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_w2`, no `_colmajor_*` anywhere), and the first
+//! version of the verify fast path dispatched the column-major kernel on it.
+//! **All five tests in this file passed throughout**, because the harness built
+//! column-major packed data to match the kernel under test:
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! $ ./target/release/kaplumba --prompt "Explain the theory of relativity." ...
+//! S l laC la own ownS l la la l own ownS l la ...
+//! $ KAPLUMBA_NO_SMALLM=1 ./target/release/kaplumba --prompt "Explain the ..."
+//! The theory of relativity is not one single theory, but two separate but
+//! related theories published by Albert Einstein ...
+//! ```
+//!
+//! Fixed structurally in two halves, both tested here: `_rm_m4` for the layout
+//! this checkpoint actually has, and `smallm_gemv_eligible`'s `perm_mode`
+//! argument, so no caller can forget which layout it is handing over. Both
+//! layouts are exercised by every test below, because a harness that only builds
+//! the data matching its kernel cannot see this class of bug at all.
+//!
+//! # What this kernel is for
+//!
+//! The speculative verify pass wants ONE pass over the packed weights for
+//! `M = K + 1` query rows. `linear_gemm_mma` cannot give that: it computes a
+//! fixed 128-query tile for any `queries`, so a 4-row batch pays 128 rows of
+//! tensor-pipe compute (`crates/kernels/src/linear.metal:10287` measures 0.33 ms
+//! of 23.4 ms surviving when the MMAs are deleted). The only way to make the
+//! verify pass cheaper than the decode pass it replaces is to reuse the decode
+//! GEMV's own access pattern with the weight read shared across rows, which is
+//! what `_2col_sk2_m4` does: one pass over `packed`, four rows of ALU.
+//!
+//! # What was believed, and what is true
+//!
+//! The design premise was that each row is **bit-identical** to running the M=1
+//! kernel on that row, and that this is what makes the port testable. Measured:
+//! that is false on the shipped build, and the reason is not an index.
+//!
+//! On the small shapes, 174-383 of 1024 columns per row differ from M=1, by 1-5
+//! fp32 ulp. Pinning the association settles it:
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! $ KAPLUMBA_NO_FAST_MATH=1 cargo test --release -p kaplumba-engine \
+//!       --test smallm_gemv_bit_exact exact
+//! 640x1024:  0/1024 columns differ   (rows and M=1 all agree bit for bit)
+//! 1408x1024: 0/1024 columns differ
+//! ```
+//!
+//! So the kernel's arithmetic **is** the M=1 arithmetic, term for term and in the
+//! same order. What differs is Metal's default fast math: the accumulator chain
+//! `p00*v[0] + p01*v[1] + ...` may be contracted and reassociated, and the
+//! compiler picks a different association once four rows of accumulators and
+//! activations are live. That is not something the source can control, and it is
+//! not something this project may switch off globally:
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! $ KAPLUMBA_NO_FAST_MATH=1 cargo test --release -p kaplumba-engine \
+//!       --test decode_matvec_golden
+//! 3bpw 5376x4096  via linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3:  3595/4096 outputs differ
+//! 3bpw 5376x5376  via linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3:  4721/5376 outputs differ
+//! 3bpw 5376x21504 via linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3: 18897/21504 outputs differ
+//! ```
+//!
+//! Disabling fast math moves the **shipped decode kernel** in ~87% of outputs,
+//! i.e. it is a decode quality change and not available. (The same switch also
+//! compiles away a real optimisation, so it is diagnostic-only in the extreme.)
+//!
+//! # Consequence for the project's "no quality loss" constraint
+//!
+//! **The row-major kernel — the one this checkpoint ships — is bit-identical to
+//! the M=1 decode kernel.** Its accumulation is spelled exactly as `linear.metal`
+//! spells it, so Metal's fast math picks the same association and the two agree
+//! to the last ulp: measured 0 ulp on all five shapes, and the four rows agree
+//! with each other. The verify pass is therefore *the decode arithmetic*, not an
+//! approximation of it, and the constraint is satisfied at the kernel level
+//! rather than argued around.
+//!
+//! Getting there required dropping the pinned `fma` chain this file originally
+//! documented. That chain was introduced for the **column-major** twin, whose
+//! body shares one `p0..p3` across four accumulators — with a plain `acc +=` its
+//! four rows disagreed with *each other*, so the association had to be pinned.
+//! The row-major kernel has no such structure (each row's four activations are
+//! its own), and pinning it was actively harmful: it bought nothing and left the
+//! kernel 1e-6 from M=1 instead of 0. That is why exactness is asserted per
+//! layout — `Layout::is_bit_exact` — rather than as one global bound.
+//!
+//! For the column-major twin, decode-greedy-identical output still cannot be
+//! *guaranteed*: a logit that differs by 1-2 ulp can flip an argmax on an exact
+//! tie. It is bounded and astronomically unlikely (the logits span ~30 after
+//! softcapping, so ties within 1e-6 do not occur on real text), and the previously
+//! committed batched verify pass used `linear_gemm_mma`, which is *further* from
+//! the decode arithmetic than either kernel is. The end-to-end acceptance check —
+//! same prompt, temperature 0, speculative vs pure decode must agree — lives with
+//! the speculative round logic; this file pins the kernel-level claim.
+//!
+//! # Sabotage check (AGENTS.md §3)
+//!
+//! A coverage claim is not a claim until the test has been seen to fail. Change
+//! one code in `dequant_4tile_2col`'s MCG constant (`0x3b603b60u` -> `0x3b603b61u`)
+//! and re-run: the ulp bound must trip with a per-row column count and a first
+//! offending column. Confirmed before commit, as required. An index bug — a
+//! wrong activation base or a mangled `red[m]` — produces errors of order the
+//! *value*, i.e. many orders of magnitude past a few hundred ulp, so the bound
+//! catches it; the bound is deliberately not "a few thousand ulp" for that reason.
+
+#[path = "common/matvec_golden.rs"]
+pub mod matvec_golden;
+
+use half::f16;
+use kaplumba_engine::exl3::{pack_integer_states, reorder_tensor_to_colmajor};
+use kaplumba_kernels::encoder::MetalEncoder;
+use kaplumba_kernels::exl3::{Exl3Codebook, Exl3Rate};
+use kaplumba_kernels::linear::{LinearEncodeRequest, LinearKernels};
+use matvec_golden::states_for;
+use metal::{Device, MTLResourceOptions};
+
+/// The M=1 kernel the column-major four-row kernel is derived from.
+///
+/// `encode_gemv_raw` dispatches this for a `perm_mode == 1` checkpoint at 3 bpw
+/// with `out_features % 32 == 0` and no `KAPLUMBA_NO_2COL`.
+const M1_CM: &str = "linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_colmajor_2col";
+
+/// The column-major four-row kernel.
+const M4_CM: &str = "linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_colmajor_2col_sk2_m4";
+
+/// The M=1 kernel the row-major four-row kernel is derived from, and the kernel
+/// this checkpoint's decode path actually dispatches.
+///
+/// `ishizuki_gemv_pipeline` prefers the two-ring-word window form for
+/// `perm_mode != 1`, and `_w2` is it. The four-row kernel is written against the
+/// three-word form (`linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3`), whose decoded states `_w2`
+/// reproduces by construction (the third word is provably never read at 3 bpw),
+/// so comparing against `_w2` checks the row loop against the *shipped* decode
+/// arithmetic rather than against a sibling variant.
+const M1_RM: &str = "linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_w2";
+
+/// The row-major four-row kernel.
+const M4_RM: &str = "linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_rm_m4";
+
+/// Rows the small-M kernel decodes in one pass.
+const ROWS: usize = 4;
+
+/// Which packed layout a case is built for.
+///
+/// The enum is the point: `run_shape` cannot be called without naming a layout,
+/// so "compare the kernel to itself on the data it expects" -- the failure mode
+/// above -- is not expressible.
+#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]
+enum Layout {
+    ColMajor,
+    RowMajor,
+}
+
+impl Layout {
+    fn all() -> [Layout; 2] {
+        [Layout::ColMajor, Layout::RowMajor]
+    }
+
+    fn perm_mode(self) -> u32 {
+        match self {
+            Layout::ColMajor => 1,
+            Layout::RowMajor => 0,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The four-row kernel under test for this layout.
+    fn m4(self) -> &'static str {
+        match self {
+            Layout::ColMajor => M4_CM,
+            Layout::RowMajor => M4_RM,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Whether the four-row kernel is expected to be **bit-identical** to the
+    /// M=1 decode kernel, and must be asserted as such.
+    ///
+    /// `RowMajor` is. Its accumulation is spelled exactly as the M=1 kernel's
+    /// (`acc += a0*v0 + a1*v1 + a2*v2 + a3*v3`), so Metal's fast math makes the
+    /// same association choice and the two agree to the last bit — measured 0 ulp
+    /// on all five shapes, and `rows_agree` true. That is the strongest form of
+    /// the project's "no quality loss" constraint: the verify pass is the decode
+    /// arithmetic, not an approximation of it.
+    ///
+    /// `ColMajor` is not, and its divergence is bounded rather than zero. Its
+    /// body shares one `p0..p3` across four accumulators and was written with an
+    /// explicit `fma` chain because plain `acc +=` made its four rows disagree
+    /// with *each other*; it still moves 174-383 of 1024 columns by a few ulp
+    /// against M=1. If that kernel is ever shipped, this is the caveat that
+    /// matters, and it is why the two are not one test.
+    fn is_bit_exact(self) -> bool {
+        matches!(self, Layout::RowMajor)
+    }
+
+    /// The M=1 kernel this layout's decode path dispatches.
+    fn m1(self) -> &'static str {
+        match self {
+            Layout::ColMajor => M1_CM,
+            Layout::RowMajor => M1_RM,
+        }
+    }
+
+    fn label(self) -> &'static str {
+        match self {
+            Layout::ColMajor => "colmajor",
+            Layout::RowMajor => "rowmajor",
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// The shapes exercised.
+///
+/// The first two are chosen for loop coverage rather than size:
+///
+/// * `640 x 1024` -- `tiles_k = 40`, so the four-trip unrolled loop runs zero
+///   times and the second loop and the tail both run.
+/// * `1408 x 1024` -- `tiles_k = 88`, so the unrolled loop runs once and the tail
+///   is still non-empty.
+///
+/// The last three are the engine's real layer projections: `5376 x 21504` is
+/// `gate_proj`/`up_proj`, `21504 x 5376` is `down_proj`, `5376 x 5376` is a
+/// sliding layer's `q_proj`. They were added after the engine emitted garbage
+/// while the two small shapes passed, to test whether the fault was
+/// shape-dependent. It was not -- and the coverage is what makes that a
+/// measurement rather than an assumption.
+///
+/// Cost is host-side packing: `5376 x 21504` is 451,584 tiles.
+const SHAPES: [(usize, usize); 5] = [
+    (640, 1024),
+    (1408, 1024),
+    (5376, 5376),
+    (5376, 21504),
+    (21504, 5376),
+];
+
+/// The divergence budget, **absolute**, in logit units.
+///
+/// Absolute is the right unit here and ulp is not. These dot products cancel: the
+/// running sum reaches ~O(10) while the result lands at ~1e-5, so a difference of
+/// a few ulp of the *running sum* is tens of thousands of ulp of the *result*
+/// while being 1e-7 in absolute terms. The ulp column in the run output is
+/// therefore not the quantity to read; `worst ... absolute` is.
+///
+/// Measured worst on this box, both layouts, all five shapes:
+///
+/// ```text
+/// colmajor 21504x5376  1.013e-6 absolute
+/// rowmajor 21504x5376  9.537e-7 absolute
+/// ```
+///
+/// i.e. it grows with `in_features` (more accumulation steps) and is worst where
+/// `K` is largest. The budget is 1e-4: **two orders above** the measured worst and
+/// one to four orders below what a real fault produces. A wrong activation base, a
+/// missing k term or a transposed row moves the result by `|p*v|`, which for these
+/// shapes is O(1) against a logit scale that is O(1) to O(30) after softcapping.
+/// A tighter bound would be fragile against a compiler or shape change; a looser
+/// one would stop catching the bug class it exists to catch.
+const ABS_BUDGET: f64 = 1e-4;
+
+/// The packed tensor in the requested layout.
+///
+/// The row-major form is the packing order itself: tile `(tk, tn)` at
+/// `(tk * tiles_n + tn) * 48` half-words. The column-major form is the same data
+/// run through `reorder_tensor_to_colmajor`, so both layouts hold identical
+/// weights and only their physical order differs -- which is what makes the two
+/// cases comparable rather than two different problems.
+fn packed_for(layout: Layout, in_f: usize, out_f: usize) -> Vec<u16> {
+    let tiles_k = in_f / 16;
+    let tiles_n = out_f / 16;
+    let mut row_major = Vec::with_capacity(tiles_k * tiles_n * 48);
+    let mut states = vec![0u16; 256];
+    for tk in 0..tiles_k {
+        for tn in 0..tiles_n {
+            for (code, slot) in states.iter_mut().enumerate() {
+                *slot = states_for(tk * tiles_n + tn, code, 3);
+            }
+            row_major.extend(pack_integer_states(&states, 3).expect("pack").0);
+        }
+    }
+    match layout {
+        Layout::RowMajor => row_major,
+        Layout::ColMajor => reorder_tensor_to_colmajor(&row_major, in_f, out_f).expect("colmajor"),
+    }
+}
+
+fn halves(values: &[f32]) -> Vec<u16> {
+    values.iter().map(|v| f16::from_f32(*v).to_bits()).collect()
+}
+
+fn buffer(device: &Device, data: &[u16]) -> metal::Buffer {
+    device.new_buffer_with_data(
+        data.as_ptr() as *const std::ffi::c_void,
+        (data.len() * 2) as u64,
+        MTLResourceOptions::StorageModeShared,
+    )
+}
+
+fn empty(device: &Device, bytes: usize) -> metal::Buffer {
+    device.new_buffer(bytes as u64, MTLResourceOptions::StorageModeShared)
+}
+
+fn read_words(buffer: &metal::Buffer, count: usize) -> Vec<u32> {
+    let ptr = buffer.contents() as *const u32;
+    (0..count)
+        .map(|i| unsafe { std::ptr::read(ptr.add(i)) })
+        .collect()
+}
+
+/// Signed-distance in ulp between two fp32 values, as raw bit patterns.
+///
+/// Zero when equal; unbounded (u64::MAX) across a sign change, because no ulp
+/// ordering exists there and a value that changed sign is not a rounding
+/// difference. Subnormals and zeros compare by their bit patterns, which is what
+/// this test needs: it is asked "did these move by a few ulp", not "are these
+/// approximately equal".
+fn ulp(a: u32, b: u32) -> u64 {
+    if a == b {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    let negative = |word: u32| word >> 31 == 1;
+    if negative(a) != negative(b) {
+        return u64::MAX;
+    }
+    u64::from(a.abs_diff(b))
+}
+
+struct ShapeResult {
+    /// (label, M=1 row, M-row row) for each row.
+    rows: Vec<(String, Vec<u32>, Vec<u32>)>,
+    /// The **finalized** fp16 output of each path, per row.
+    ///
+    /// Separate from `rows` because `raw` matching does not imply `output`
+    /// matching: the M=1 path finalizes with `linear_finalize_simd` and the
+    /// four-row path with `linear_finalize_rows`, two different kernels that are
+    /// supposed to be the same transform written two ways. Nothing checked that
+    /// until this field existed, and the engine reads `output`.
+    outputs: Vec<(String, Vec<u16>, Vec<u16>)>,
+    /// The transformed activations, `[rows][in_features]` flattened, from each path.
+    m1_transformed: Vec<u32>,
+    smallm_transformed: Vec<u32>,
+    /// Worst ulp distance from M=1 seen on any output of any row.
+    worst: u64,
+    worst_at: String,
+    /// Worst absolute difference from M=1, and where. This is the quantity the
+    /// budget is stated in.
+    worst_abs: f64,
+    worst_abs_at: String,
+}
+
+/// `identical_rows` gives all four rows the same activations. That is a
+/// discriminator, not a convenience: it asks whether the kernel is a function of
+/// its inputs per row, independently of the row it is on. Under fast math it is
+/// **not** guaranteed and this test reports rather than asserts it.
+fn run_shape(
+    device: &Device,
+    kernels: &LinearKernels,
+    in_f: usize,
+    out_f: usize,
+    identical_rows: bool,
+    layout: Layout,
+) -> ShapeResult {
+    let queue = device.new_command_queue();
+    let packed = packed_for(layout, in_f, out_f);
+    let packed_b = buffer(device, &packed);
+    let perm_mode = layout.perm_mode();
+    let m1_name = layout.m1();
+    let m4_name = layout.m4();
+
+    let rows: Vec<Vec<u16>> = (0..ROWS)
+        .map(|m| {
+            halves(
+                &(0..in_f)
+                    .map(|i| {
+                        let x = (i + 977 * m) as f32;
+                        (x * 0.0173).sin() + (x * 0.0031).cos() + 0.25 * (m as f32 + 1.0)
+                    })
+                    .collect::<Vec<f32>>(),
+            )
+        })
+        .collect();
+    let rows: Vec<Vec<u16>> = if identical_rows {
+        vec![rows[0].clone(); ROWS]
+    } else {
+        rows
+    };
+    let mut flat = Vec::with_capacity(ROWS * in_f);
+    for row in &rows {
+        flat.extend_from_slice(row);
+    }
+    let input_b = buffer(device, &flat);
+
+    let input_signs_b = buffer(
+        device,
+        &halves(
+            &(0..in_f)
+                .map(|i| if i % 3 == 0 { 0.0021 } else { -0.0017 })
+                .collect::<Vec<f32>>(),
+        ),
+    );
+    let output_signs_b = buffer(
+        device,
+        &halves(
+            &(0..out_f)
+                .map(|i| if i % 5 == 0 { -0.0031 } else { 0.0023 })
+                .collect::<Vec<f32>>(),
+        ),
+    );
+
+    let transformed = empty(device, in_f * 2);
+    let raw = empty(device, out_f * 4);
+    let output = empty(device, out_f * 2);
+
+    let mut m1: Vec<Vec<u32>> = Vec::with_capacity(ROWS);
+    let mut m1_out: Vec<Vec<u16>> = Vec::with_capacity(ROWS);
+    let mut m1_transformed: Vec<u32> = Vec::with_capacity(ROWS * in_f / 2);
+    for (m, row) in rows.iter().enumerate() {
+        let row_b = buffer(device, row);
+        let mut encoder = MetalEncoder::new(&queue).expect("encoder");
+        kernels
+            .encode_gemv_named(
+                &mut encoder,
+                m1_name,
+                &LinearEncodeRequest {
+                    packed: &packed_b,
+                    packed_offset: 0,
+                    perm_mode,
+                    input: &row_b,
+                    input_signs: &input_signs_b,
+                    output_signs: &output_signs_b,
+                    transformed: &transformed,
+                    raw: &raw,
+                    output: &output,
+                    in_features: in_f,
+                    out_features: out_f,
+                    rate: Exl3Rate::Integer(3),
+                    codebook: Exl3Codebook::Mcg,
+                    pretransformed: false,
+                },
+            )
+            .expect("m1 dispatch");
+        encoder.commit_and_wait().expect("m1 commit");
+        let got = read_words(&raw, out_f);
+        assert!(
+            got.iter().any(|v| *v != 0),
+            "{m1_name} produced an all-zero row {m} at {in_f}x{out_f} ({}); the kernel did not \
+             run, so comparing against the M{ROWS} kernel would be comparing nothing to nothing",
+            layout.label()
+        );
+        m1.push(got);
+        let out_ptr = output.contents() as *const u16;
+        m1_out.push(
+            (0..out_f)
+                .map(|i| unsafe { std::ptr::read(out_ptr.add(i)) })
+                .collect(),
+        );
+        m1_transformed.extend(read_words(&transformed, in_f / 2));
+    }
+
+    let transformed_rows = empty(device, ROWS * in_f * 2);
+    let raw_rows = empty(device, ROWS * out_f * 4);
+    let output_rows = empty(device, ROWS * out_f * 2);
+    let mut encoder = MetalEncoder::new(&queue).expect("encoder");
+    kernels
+        .encode_gemv_smallm(
+            &mut encoder,
+            &LinearEncodeRequest {
+                packed: &packed_b,
+                packed_offset: 0,
+                perm_mode,
+                input: &input_b,
+                input_signs: &input_signs_b,
+                output_signs: &output_signs_b,
+                transformed: &transformed_rows,
+                raw: &raw_rows,
+                output: &output_rows,
+                in_features: in_f,
+                out_features: out_f,
+                rate: Exl3Rate::Integer(3),
+                codebook: Exl3Codebook::Mcg,
+                pretransformed: false,
+            },
+            ROWS,
+        )
+        .unwrap_or_else(|e| {
+            panic!(
+                "smallm dispatch of {m4_name} at {in_f}x{out_f} ({}) failed: {e}",
+                layout.label()
+            )
+        });
+    encoder.commit_and_wait().expect("smallm commit");
+    let all = read_words(&raw_rows, ROWS * out_f);
+    let all_out: Vec<u16> = {
+        let ptr = output_rows.contents() as *const u16;
+        (0..ROWS * out_f)
+            .map(|i| unsafe { std::ptr::read(ptr.add(i)) })
+            .collect()
+    };
+
+    let mut worst = 0u64;
+    let mut worst_at = String::new();
+    let mut worst_abs = 0.0f64;
+    let mut worst_abs_at = String::new();
+    let rows: Vec<(String, Vec<u32>, Vec<u32>)> = (0..ROWS)
+        .map(|m| {
+            let got = all[m * out_f..(m + 1) * out_f].to_vec();
+            for (column, (a, b)) in m1[m].iter().zip(got.iter()).enumerate() {
+                let distance = ulp(*a, *b);
+                let here = format!("{in_f}x{out_f} row {m} column {column} ({})", layout.label());
+                if distance > worst {
+                    worst = distance;
+                    worst_at = here.clone();
+                }
+                let delta =
+                    (f32::from_bits(*a) as f64 - f32::from_bits(*b) as f64).abs();
+                if delta > worst_abs {
+                    worst_abs = delta;
+                    worst_abs_at = here;
+                }
+            }
+            (format!("{} {in_f}x{out_f} row {m}", layout.label()), m1[m].clone(), got)
+        })
+        .collect();
+
+    let outputs = (0..ROWS)
+        .map(|m| {
+            (
+                format!("{} {in_f}x{out_f} row {m}", layout.label()),
+                m1_out[m].clone(),
+                all_out[m * out_f..(m + 1) * out_f].to_vec(),
+            )
+        })
+        .collect();
+
+    ShapeResult {
+        rows,
+        outputs,
+        m1_transformed,
+        smallm_transformed: read_words(&transformed_rows, ROWS * in_f / 2),
+        worst,
+        worst_at,
+        worst_abs,
+        worst_abs_at,
+    }
+}
+
+/// The input transform half of the contract. This one *is* exactly enforceable.
+///
+/// The M=1 path runs `linear_input_transform` (`transform_simd`, a simdgroup
+/// butterfly) and the four-row path runs `linear_input_rows` (`transform_vector`,
+/// a linear butterfly). They are the same transform written two ways, and unlike
+/// the accumulation they are only adds and subtracts of two operands with no
+/// contraction opportunity, so fast math cannot move them. Measured bit-identical
+/// on every row of both shapes, with fast math on and off.
+#[test]
+fn the_two_input_transforms_agree_bit_for_bit() {
+    let Some(device) = Device::system_default() else {
+        return;
+    };
+    let kernels = LinearKernels::shared().expect("kernels");
+    let mut compared = 0usize;
+    for layout in Layout::all() {
+        for (in_f, out_f) in SHAPES {
+            let r = run_shape(&device, &kernels, in_f, out_f, false, layout);
+            let bad = r
+                .m1_transformed
+                .iter()
+                .zip(r.smallm_transformed.iter())
+                .filter(|(a, b)| a != b)
+                .count();
+            assert_eq!(
+                bad,
+                0,
+                "{} {in_f}x{out_f}: the two input transforms differ in {bad}/{} half-words. \
+                 `linear_input_transform` and `linear_input_rows` are the same butterfly in the same \
+                 order, so a difference means one of them was edited, or one of them reads the input \
+                 or its signs at a different base.",
+                layout.label(),
+                r.m1_transformed.len()
+            );
+            compared += r.m1_transformed.len();
+        }
+    }
+    assert!(compared > 0, "no transform words were compared");
+}
+
+/// The bound that stands in for bit-exactness on the shipped build, **plus** the
+/// unconditional exactness assertion for the layouts that really are exact.
+///
+/// A layout whose `is_bit_exact()` is true must show 0 ulp here, on every row of
+/// every shape, with fast math on. That is a stronger requirement than the budget
+/// and it is stated as an equality, not a bound — if a future edit to the
+/// row-major kernel's accumulation moves anything, this fails without needing the
+/// budget to be reinterpreted.
+#[test]
+fn every_row_stays_within_the_fp32_budget_of_the_m1_gemv() {
+    let Some(device) = Device::system_default() else {
+        return;
+    };
+    let kernels = LinearKernels::shared().expect("kernels");
+    let mut over: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
+    let mut inexact: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
+    let mut compared = 0usize;
+    for layout in Layout::all() {
+        assert!(
+            kernels.ishizuki_gemv_pipeline_by_name(layout.m4()).is_some(),
+            "{} is absent from the compiled family, so the {} case would pass on an empty \
+             comparison",
+            layout.m4(),
+            layout.label()
+        );
+        for (in_f, out_f) in SHAPES {
+            let r = run_shape(&device, &kernels, in_f, out_f, false, layout);
+            if layout.is_bit_exact() && r.worst != 0 {
+                inexact.push(format!(
+                    "{} {}x{}: {} ulp, worst {:.3e} absolute at {}",
+                    layout.label(),
+                    in_f,
+                    out_f,
+                    r.worst,
+                    r.worst_abs,
+                    r.worst_abs_at
+                ));
+            }
+            for (label, want, got) in &r.rows {
+                compared += 1;
+                let worst = want
+                    .iter()
+                    .zip(got.iter())
+                    .map(|(a, b)| (f32::from_bits(*a) as f64 - f32::from_bits(*b) as f64).abs())
+                    .fold(0.0f64, f64::max);
+                if worst > ABS_BUDGET {
+                    let first = want
+                        .iter()
+                        .zip(got.iter())
+                        .position(|(a, b)| {
+                            (f32::from_bits(*a) as f64 - f32::from_bits(*b) as f64).abs()
+                                > ABS_BUDGET
+                        })
+                        .unwrap_or(0);
+                    over.push(format!(
+                        "{label}: worst {worst:e}, first over budget at column {first} \
+                         ({:08x} vs {:08x})",
+                        want[first], got[first]
+                    ));
+                }
+            }
+            println!(
+                "{} {in_f}x{out_f}: worst {:.3e} absolute ({} ulp) at {}",
+                layout.label(),
+                r.worst_abs,
+                r.worst,
+                r.worst_abs_at
+            );
+        }
+    }
+    assert_eq!(
+        compared,
+        SHAPES.len() * ROWS * Layout::all().len(),
+        "{compared} rows were compared; {} shapes x {ROWS} rows x {} layouts is {} and anything \
+         less means a case stopped being exercised",
+        SHAPES.len(),
+        Layout::all().len(),
+        SHAPES.len() * ROWS * Layout::all().len()
+    );
+    assert!(
+        inexact.is_empty(),
+        "the row-major four-row GEMV spells its accumulation exactly as the M=1 kernel does, so \
+         fast math must pick the same association and the two must agree to the last bit. A \
+         non-zero ulp count here means the accumulation was edited away from that form, or a \
+         weight/activation read moved -- and it is the difference between a bit-exact verify pass \
+         and a bounded one. Do not weaken this to a budget.\n\n{}",
+        inexact.join("\n")
+    );
+    assert!(
+        over.is_empty(),
+        "a row of the four-row GEMV is more than {ABS_BUDGET:e} from the M=1 GEMV in absolute \
+         terms. The budget covers the difference between the M=1 helper's rounding sequence and \
+         the four-row kernel's pinned one (measured worst: 1.0e-6 at 21504x5376, i.e. two orders \
+         of headroom), so exceeding it means something other than rounding moved: an activation \
+         base off by a row or a 16-element group, a mangled `red[m]` dimension, or the wrong \
+         packed word.\n\n{}",
+        over.join("\n")
+    );
+}
+
+/// The gate that keeps the four-row family off layouts it cannot decode, and the
+/// layout-to-kernel map that stops it picking the wrong twin.
+///
+/// This test exists because its absence cost a full end-to-end debugging cycle.
+/// The shipped checkpoint's layer linears are **row-major**
+/// (`permutation_mode == 0` for all 411 tensors, see `KAPLUMBA_SMALLM_LOG`), and
+/// the first version of the verify fast path pointed the **column-major** kernel
+/// at them. That did not fail — it decoded the wrong weights and the model
+/// emitted noise. Every test in this file passed throughout, because they built
+/// column-major packed data to match the kernel under test.
+///
+/// The lesson is in the signature: `perm_mode` is an argument here rather than a
+/// caller-side convention, and `smallm_gemv_kernel_for` turns it into the kernel
+/// name in one place, so no caller can forget which layout it is handing over.
+#[test]
+fn the_smallm_family_is_gated_to_the_two_layouts_it_can_decode() {
+    let eligible = |perm_mode: u32, rate: Exl3Rate, rows: usize| {
+        LinearKernels::smallm_gemv_eligible(rate, Exl3Codebook::Mcg, perm_mode, 5376, 8192, rows)
+    };
+    for perm_mode in [0u32, 1] {
+        assert!(
+            eligible(perm_mode, Exl3Rate::Integer(3), ROWS),
+            "perm_mode {perm_mode} is one of the two layouts with a four-row kernel; if this \
+             gate refuses it the kernel is dead code"
+        );
+    }
+    for (label, perm_mode, rate, rows) in [
+        ("an unknown layout", 2u32, Exl3Rate::Integer(3), ROWS),
+        ("an unknown layout", 3, Exl3Rate::Integer(3), ROWS),
+        ("three rows", 1, Exl3Rate::Integer(3), 3),
+        ("one row", 1, Exl3Rate::Integer(3), 1),
+        ("six bpw", 1, Exl3Rate::Integer(6), ROWS),
+    ] {
+        assert!(
+            !eligible(perm_mode, rate, rows),
+            "{label} (perm_mode {perm_mode}) must not reach a four-row kernel"
+        );
+    }
+
+    // The map must pair each layout with its own kernel. Swapping these two is
+    // exactly the shipped bug: both are eligible, both exist, and only one
+    // decodes the weights.
+    assert_eq!(LinearKernels::smallm_gemv_kernel_for(0), M4_RM);
+    assert_eq!(LinearKernels::smallm_gemv_kernel_for(1), M4_CM);
+    assert_ne!(
+        LinearKernels::smallm_gemv_kernel_for(0),
+        LinearKernels::smallm_gemv_kernel_for(1),
+        "the two layouts need two kernels; one name for both is the bug this gate exists for"
+    );
+}
+
+/// The **finalized** output, not just the raw accumulator.
+///
+/// This is the assertion whose absence let a garbage model run through a green
+/// test suite. `raw` matching does not imply `output` matching: the M=1 path
+/// finalizes with `linear_finalize_simd` (a `simd_shuffle_xor` butterfly over
+/// four registers per lane) and the four-row path with `linear_finalize_rows` (a
+/// linear `transform_vector` over 128 values per thread). They are supposed to be
+/// the same transform written two ways, and the engine reads `output`.
+///
+/// # Why this is a bound and not equality
+///
+/// For the **row-major** kernel the two are bit-identical all the way through,
+/// so this assertion is an equality in disguise; for the **column-major** twin
+/// they differ where the fp32 divergence ([`ABS_BUDGET`], measured 6e-8) tips a
+/// value across an fp16 rounding boundary — 12-14 of 21504 columns on
+/// `5376 x 21504`, i.e. 0.06%, each off by exactly one fp16 ulp.
+///
+/// So the assertion is on the two things a **layout** fault would break and
+/// rounding cannot: the *fraction* of columns that move, and the *magnitude*.
+/// Passing the wrong `raw` layout, the wrong `out_features`, or reading the
+/// activations for the wrong row moves ~half the columns and by O(1), not 0.06%
+/// of them by 1e-6.
+#[test]
+fn the_finalized_output_matches_the_m1_path() {
+    let Some(device) = Device::system_default() else {
+        return;
+    };
+    let kernels = LinearKernels::shared().expect("kernels");
+    let mut mismatches: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
+    let mut compared = 0usize;
+    for layout in Layout::all() {
+        for (in_f, out_f) in SHAPES {
+            let r = run_shape(&device, &kernels, in_f, out_f, false, layout);
+            for (label, want, got) in &r.outputs {
+                compared += 1;
+                let bad: Vec<usize> = want
+                    .iter()
+                    .zip(got.iter())
+                    .enumerate()
+                    .filter(|(_, (a, b))| a != b)
+                    .map(|(i, _)| i)
+                    .collect();
+                let worst = want
+                    .iter()
+                    .zip(got.iter())
+                    .map(|(a, b)| {
+                        (f16::from_bits(*a).to_f32() as f64 - f16::from_bits(*b).to_f32() as f64).abs()
+                    })
+                    .fold(0.0f64, f64::max);
+                // 1% of columns, ten times the measured 0.06%, and four orders of
+                // magnitude below the ~50% a layout fault moves.
+                if bad.len() * 100 > want.len() || worst > ABS_BUDGET {
+                    mismatches.push(format!(
+                        "{label}: {}/{} half-words differ ({:.2}% of columns), worst {worst:e}, \
+                         first at column {} ({:04x} vs {:04x})",
+                        bad.len(),
+                        want.len(),
+                        100.0 * bad.len() as f64 / want.len() as f64,
+                        bad.first().copied().unwrap_or(0),
+                        want[bad.first().copied().unwrap_or(0)],
+                        got[bad.first().copied().unwrap_or(0)],
+                    ));
+                }
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    assert_eq!(
+        compared,
+        SHAPES.len() * ROWS * Layout::all().len(),
+        "{compared} rows of finalized output were compared; {} shapes x {ROWS} rows x {} layouts \
+         is {}",
+        SHAPES.len(),
+        Layout::all().len(),
+        SHAPES.len() * ROWS * Layout::all().len()
+    );
+    assert!(
+        mismatches.is_empty(),
+        "the four-row kernel's finalized output is not the M=1 path's within the fp32 budget and a \
+         1% column budget. The two paths finalize with different kernels \
+         (`linear_finalize_simd` vs `linear_finalize_rows`) which are supposed to be the same \
+         transform written two ways; exceeding this means they are not, or that the small-M path \
+         passes the wrong `raw` layout or the wrong `out_features` to its finalize.\n\n{}",
+        mismatches.join("\n")
+    );
+}
+
+/// The exact claim, which needs the association pinned.
+///
+/// Runs only when `KAPLUMBA_NO_FAST_MATH` is set, because that is the only way to
+/// stop the compiler reassociating a chain the source spells out. It cannot be the
+/// default: the same switch moves the shipped M=1 decode kernel by ~87% of its
+/// outputs (`decode_matvec_golden`), which is a decode quality change.
+///
+/// Also reports whether the four rows agree with each other on identical input,
+/// which is the property that licenses the `MR` row loop. Under fast math that is
+/// the compiler's choice per row and not guaranteed, so it is reported and only
+/// asserted when the association is pinned.
+#[test]
+fn the_four_row_kernel_is_the_m1_kernel_when_fast_math_is_off() {
+    let Some(device) = Device::system_default() else {
+        return;
+    };
+    let pinned = std::env::var_os("KAPLUMBA_NO_FAST_MATH").is_some();
+    let kernels = LinearKernels::shared().expect("kernels");
+
+    for layout in Layout::all() {
+        for (in_f, out_f) in SHAPES {
+            let r = run_shape(&device, &kernels, in_f, out_f, true, layout);
+            let m4: Vec<&Vec<u32>> = r.rows.iter().map(|(_, _, got)| got).collect();
+            let rows_agree = m4.windows(2).all(|w| w[0] == w[1]);
+            println!(
+                "{} {in_f}x{out_f}: rows_agree={rows_agree} worst={:.3e} abs / {} ulp{}",
+                layout.label(),
+                r.worst_abs,
+                r.worst,
+                if pinned { " (fast math off)" } else { "" }
+            );
+            // The row-major kernel is exact with fast math on, so it is held to
+            // this unconditionally. The column-major twin is only exact once the
+            // association is pinned, so it is reported and skipped otherwise.
+            if !pinned && !layout.is_bit_exact() {
+                continue;
+            }
+            assert!(
+                rows_agree,
+                "{} {in_f}x{out_f}: four rows given one activation vector produced different outputs, \
+                 so the row dimension is not a function of its input",
+                layout.label()
+            );
+            assert_eq!(
+                r.worst, 0,
+                "{} {in_f}x{out_f}: with the fp association pinned, every row must be bit-identical \
+                 to the M=1 kernel, and the worst divergence is {} ulp at {}",
+                layout.label(),
+                r.worst,
+                r.worst_at
+            );
+        }
+    }
+}
