+# Implementation Notes: Sustained $\ge 30\text{ tok/s}$ Speculative Generation up to 16k Context
+
+## 1. Initial State & Empirical Ground Truth
+- Target Model: Gemma 4 31B (`Gxma-repacked`, EXL3 ~3 bpw, 12.055 GB weights).
+- Assistant Drafter: `~/.omlx/models/mlx-community/gemma-4-31B-it-qat-assistant-4bit` (0.28 GB, 4-bit affine).
+- M4 Pro Hardware: 20 GPU cores, 24 GiB unified RAM, 273.0 GB/s peak memory bus.
+- Physical roofline for pure single-token decode: 14.5–14.9 tok/s (180.2 GB/s). Pure decode cannot exceed 17.3–18.2 tok/s at 0 ms overhead due to the 273 GB/s bus ceiling.
+- Verified acceptance gate with resident drafter:
+  - `"Explain the theory of relativity."`: 2.133 tokens/pass $\rightarrow$ 23.84 tok/s.
+  - `"Explain quantum computing in detail:"`: 2.700 tokens/pass $\rightarrow$ 30.17 tok/s.
+  - At $K=5$, acceptance reaches up to 2.20–2.80 tokens/pass.
+
+## 2. T8a batched verify — implemented, and **blocked by the M>1 GEMM kernel**
+
+`TextModel::forward_verify_rows` (`crates/engine/src/model.rs`) runs `[pending, drafts...]`
+(M = K+1) through the layer stack with `run_head=false`, applies the final norm to every row
+on the GPU, runs the **vocabulary head as one multi-query GEMM** (`linear_gemm_mma`), softcaps
+and returns `(hidden_rows, logits_rows)`. `speculative.rs` drives one such pass per round,
+accepts the longest matching draft prefix, emits `accepted + 1` (bonus) tokens, retracts the
+rejected KV tail and reseeds the drafter from the verify row that produced the bonus — one
+target pass per round, no per-draft target forward.
+
+It runs and produces coherent text, **but it is slower than the sequential version**, and the
+reason is not our code:
+
+| measurement (`Gxma-repacked`, 60 layers) | value |
+|---|---|
+| decode (M=1) GEMV weight streaming | ~180 GB/s, ~1.1 ms/layer, **14.1 tok/s** |
+| M>1 (`linear_gemm_mma` or the scalar fallback) | **~1.2 s per pass, 60 layers @ ~20 ms each** |
+| M=2 vs M=78 per-layer GPU time | **identical (~20 ms)** |
+
+Root cause (corrected from an earlier "10 GB/s weight-bound" reading, which was **wrong**):
+`linear_gemm_mma` processes a **fixed 128-query × 64-output tile** (`QTILE=128`, 8 simdgroups of
+16 rows) regardless of `params.queries`, and its own header records that deleting all sixteen
+MMAs leaves **0.33 ms of 23.4 ms** — i.e. **~99% of the runtime is the tensor pipe on the padded
+tile** (`crates/kernels/src/linear.metal:10287`). So *every* M from 1 to 128 pays the full
+128-row MMA compute: M=K+1≤9 wastes ~93–99% of it. That is the whole cost, and it is why M=2 and
+M=78 are indistinguishable. `linear_gemm_mma_4sg` / `_regpipe` are also 128-row tiles, and
+`KAPLUMBA_DISABLE_MMA_PREFILL=1` (scalar tiled arm) is no better (12–48 ms/layer, high variance).
+
+Consequence for the design: a K-draft verify costs **1.2 s**, not the ~67–89 ms a single
+decode pass costs, so `accepted + 1 ≈ 2.5` tokens per 1.2 s ≈ **2 tok/s** — 6× *worse* than pure
+decode. There is **no small-M kernel family** in `crates/kernels/src/linear*.metal`: the GEMV
+family is M=1 only, and every GEMM is M≥128-tiled.
+
+### What is actually required for ≥ 30 tok/s
+
+1. **A small-M (≤8) colmajor GEMV kernel that shares one weight dequant across the M rows.**
+   The decode GEMV's dequant (`decode_and_accum_4tile_2col`, `linear_repacked.metal:65`) is
+   already in registers and already vectorized to 180 GB/s, but it fuses dequant *with*
+   accumulation, so an M-row version must **split the dequant out of the helper** (dequant once
+   → `v[32]`, then accumulate M times against M activation rows). Per-row arithmetic is then
+   identical to the M=1 kernel's, so each row is **bit-exact** to running the existing kernel M
+   times — the one correctness property that makes the port testable. `decode_and_accum_4tile_2col`
+   is called by the shipped `_2col_sk2` / `_2col` unrolled loops; the split must be additive
+   (new helper), never an edit to those.
+
+   Integration (bigger than the kernel): route `tokens in 2..=M` in `DecoderLayer::encode_linear`
+   to the new kernel for **all seven** projections **plus the lm_head**, and make the M≤8 attention
+   path match. Attention for M≤8 currently goes through the prefill kernels, which are also
+   tuned for large M.
+
+   Expected payoff is **not certain**: M rows share one weight read, but the ALU per weight rises
+   M×. `spec/alu-boost` found 4-way independent evaluation hides the ALU latency at M=1; whether
+   M=4 lands at ~1.1× or ~4× the M=1 pass (67 ms) decides between ~30 tok/s and ~8 tok/s. Measure
+   the kernel standalone before wiring it in.
+2. **A ~30× faster drafter forward.** With the batched round the drafter still costs
+**193 ms for K=4 (48 ms/draft)**: `DrafterModel::gemv_named` allocates a fresh output buffer and
+dispatches one GEMV **per projection** (28 per draft step), each with its own command buffer +
+wait. Target is ~2 ms/draft by encoding all projections of a step into one command buffer with
+pre-allocated buffers.
+3. **Shared-KV extraction** (`SpeculativeEngine::extract_shared_kv`) re-reads the last full
+layer's KV to the host every round: ~268 MB/round at 16k (16×512×16k×2 B), which will not
+survive 30 rounds/s. Needs to stay device-resident or be incremental.
+
+Measured this session: pure decode 14.13 tok/s; sequential spec 2.5–7.5 tok/s; batched spec
+2.49 tok/s with `KAPLUMBA_SPEC_TRACE=1` showing `verify=1279 ms` and `draft=193 ms` per round.
+
+## 3. T8b four-row verify GEMV — built, verified, and **mis-targeted at the packing family**
+
+### 3.1 What was added
+
+| file | change |
+|---|---|
+| `crates/kernels/src/linear_repacked.metal` | `dequant_4tile_2col` (the fused helper's dequant, split out verbatim), `dot4_contracted` (a four-term dot with the rounding sequence pinned), `accum_row_4tile_2col` (explicit `fma`, so nothing is reassociable), macros `KAPLUMBA_VERIFY_ROW` / `KAPLUMBA_VERIFY_ROWS4`, kernel `linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_colmajor_2col_sk2_m4` (`MR = 4`, one weight read for four rows). |
+| `crates/kernels/src/linear.rs` | `SMALLM_GEMV_KERNEL`, `SMALLM_GEMV_ROWS = 4`, `smallm_gemv_eligible(rate, codebook, perm_mode, in, out, rows)`, `encode_gemv_smallm`. |
+| `crates/engine/src/model.rs` | routes `tokens == 4` in `encode_linear` and the head in `forward_verify_rows` to `encode_gemv_smallm`. Diagnostics: `KAPLUMBA_NO_SMALLM`, `KAPLUMBA_SMALLM_LOG`, `KAPLUMBA_SMALLM_SKIP`. |
+| `crates/engine/tests/smallm_gemv_bit_exact.rs` | new, 5 tests; this is the file the kernel doc comment had been referring to before it existed. |
+
+### 3.2 Measured on hardware (M4 Pro, Gxma)
+
+- `rows=4 layers(encode)=31.9 ms` per verify round, against **1280 ms** through `linear_gemm_mma`. The small-M kernel is real.
+- `commit+readback ≈ 152 ms` is the actual GPU time of the 4-row pass; the 31.9 ms is encode (CPU submit), not execution. Do not quote the 31.9 ms as the pass cost.
+- **`rows=3` / `rows=2` fall off the `rows == 4` gate onto the MMA path and cost 1280 ms.** The drafter shrinks `k` after a rejection, so a trace shows a 7x cliff dominating the run. Padding the batch to 4 rows is the obvious fix and is not yet done.
+- `kv ≈ 0.5 ms/round`, `draft ≈ 99 ms/round` (the 28-dispatch-per-step drafter forward, unaddressed).
+
+### 3.3 The kernel is correct. Three claims, all measured, not assumed
+
+```
+$ cargo test --release -p kaplumba-engine --test smallm_gemv_bit_exact
+5 passed
+```
+- **The input transform is bit-identical** to the M=1 path's on every row of five shapes (the M=1 path runs `linear_input_transform`/`transform_simd`; the four-row path runs `linear_input_rows`/`transform_vector`). Only adds and subtracts, so fast math cannot move them.
+- **The four rows agree with each other** on one identical activation vector, so the row loop, `red[m]` and the `x + m * K` base are exact. This was *false* with the original `acc += p*v + ...` body and became true once the accumulation used an explicit `fma` chain.
+- **Worst divergence from M=1: 5.96e-8 absolute** (1344 raw ulp; these dots cancel, so ulp-of-the-result overstates it by 1000x — the budget is absolute for that reason).
+- **With the association pinned, 0 ulp on all five shapes**: `KAPLUMBA_NO_FAST_MATH=1 cargo test ... --test smallm_gemv_bit_exact`. That is what proves the divergence is reassociation rather than a wrong index.
+
+`KAPLUMBA_NO_FAST_MATH=1` **cannot ship**: it moves the *shipped* decode kernel (`linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3`) in ~87% of outputs (`decode_matvec_golden`: 3595/4096, 4721/5376, 18897/21504). It exists only as the instrument that attributes a divergence to reassociation. Metal's default fast math reassociates the accumulator chain differently once four rows of accumulators and activations are live; explicit `fma` is the way to pin it locally.
+
+Sabotage check (AGENTS.md §3): changing row 3's activation base from `3u * K` to `2u * K` makes `every_row_stays_within_the_fp32_budget_of_the_m1_gemv` fail with **row 3 only**, worst 0.33 / 0.52 against a 1e-4 budget — ~5000x of separation. Confirmed and reverted.
+
+### 3.4 Why the model still emitted garbage — the kernel was reading the wrong family
+
+**This checkpoint's 411 tensors are ROW-MAJOR.** `KAPLUMBA_SMALLM_LOG=1` prints `perm=0` for every one, and `KAPLUMBA_TRACE_GEMV=1` shows the M=1 decode kernel is **`linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_w2`** — no `_colmajor_*` anywhere. The four-row kernel is **column-major**, derived from `_2col`.
+
+Pointing a column-major kernel at row-major weights does not fault. It decodes the wrong numbers and the model emits noise (`"S l laC la own ownS l la la l own..."`), which is exactly the symptom that started this thread. A reference run with `KAPLUMBA_NO_SMALLM=1` is coherent on the same prompt (`"...theory of relativity is not one single theory, but two separate but related theories published by Albert Einstein..."`), which localised the fault to this path in one A/B.
+
+**All five unit tests passed throughout**, because the harness builds column-major packed data to match the kernel. A test that constructs its own input cannot see that the *model* disagrees with it. Fixed structurally: `smallm_gemv_eligible` now takes `perm_mode` and refuses 0, so the gate is an argument no caller can omit, and `the_four_row_kernel_is_gated_off_for_row_major_tensors` pins it.
+
+Consequence: **on the shipped model the speculative verify pass still uses `encode_gemm`**, measured 1.49-2.57 tok/s. The kernel is a correct, tested, unused artifact until it is re-derived for the row-major family.
+
+### 3.5 What is actually needed next
+
+1. **A row-major four-row kernel**, derived from `linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3` / `_w2` (`crates/kernels/src/linear.metal:1822`) rather than `_2col`. The recipe and the measured properties above transfer; only the packed addressing and the dequant differ. `smallm_gemv_bit_exact.rs` is the harness — point it at row-major packing and the `_k3` name. Alternatively vendor a column-major repack of the checkpoint (`repack_model.rs` exists), which also buys the colmajor family's measured bandwidth.
+2. Pad the verify batch to exactly 4 rows (or generalise `MR`), to remove the `rows=3`/`rows=2` 1280 ms cliff.
+3. The drafter forward is still ~99 ms/round for 28 dispatches per step; target ~2 ms.
+
+## 4. T8c — the row-major four-row kernel, and why the layout gate is now a function
+
+### 4.1 What was wrong with §3
+
+§3.4 identified the mis-target and fixed it by *refusing* `perm_mode == 0`. That fix made the
+kernel safe but left it **dead**: `KAPLUMBA_SMALLM_LOG=1` reports `perm=0` for all 411 tensors, so
+every verify round fell off the gate onto `linear_gemm_mma` at ~1280 ms. Refusing the layout is not
+the same as supporting it.
+
+### 4.2 The row-major kernel
+
+`linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_rm_m4` (`crates/kernels/src/linear_repacked.metal`, appended at EOF) is
+`linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3` (`crates/kernels/src/linear.metal:1822`) with the k-tile loop repeated for
+`MR = 4` rows. Everything that is part of the *arithmetic* is copied verbatim:
+
+- the k-tile partition: simdgroup `sg` takes tiles `sg, sg+8, sg+16, ...`;
+- the reduction: `simd_shuffle_xor 1` then `2`, then `red[m][sg][lane/4]` and the `g = 0..7` walk of
+  `red[m][g][c]`;
+- the 3-word window `j0/j1/j2` + `sh0` and the `r = i*KA + 16` bit extraction;
+- the unroll-by-2 structure and the `tk += SG` tail.
+
+**Addressing (this is the whole difference from `_2col`):** the row-major pack holds tile `(tk, tn)`
+at `(tk * tiles_n + tn) * WORDS`, so the kernel uses `column = packed + tn * WORDS` and
+`stride = tiles_n * WORDS`, stepping `tk` by `stride`. The column-major family reverses the outer
+two axes (tile at `(tn * tiles_k + tk) * WORDS`), which is why the two are not interchangeable.
+The variable is still *named* `column` in the original kernel; that name is a leftover and does not
+mean the kernel is column-major. `KAPLUMBA_TRACE_GEMV=1` + `KAPLUMBA_SMALLM_LOG=1`, not the names,
+are what decide the layout.
+
+Accumulation goes through `dot4_contracted` (`t = p0*v0; t = fma(p1,v1,t); ...`) rather than
+`acc += p0*v0 + p1*v1 + ...`, because that is what stopped §3's kernel reassociating once four rows
+of accumulators were live.
+
+### 4.3 The Rust side is now layout-aware in one place
+
+| item | change |
+|---|---|
+| `SMALLM_GEMV_KERNEL` | still `linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_colmajor_2col_sk2_m4` (`perm_mode == 1`) |
+| `SMALLM_GEMV_KERNEL_RM` | new: `linear_gemv_ishizuki_k3_rm_m4` (`perm_mode == 0`) |
+| `smallm_gemv_eligible` | now `matches!(perm_mode, 0 \| 1)` — 2+ is still refused |
+| `smallm_gemv_kernel_for(perm_mode)` | new; the only place a layout becomes a kernel name |
+
+A caller cannot swap the twins any more: `encode_gemv_smallm` derives the name from
+`request.perm_mode`. `the_smallm_family_is_gated_to_the_two_layouts_it_can_decode` asserts the map
+pairs each layout with its own kernel *and* that the two names differ, because "both eligible, both
+exist, only one decodes the weights" is precisely the shipped bug.
+
+### 4.4 Measured (M4 Pro, both layouts, 5 shapes x 4 rows)
+
+```
+colmajor 640x1024   worst 4.470e-8 abs (1344 ulp)
+colmajor 1408x1024  worst 5.960e-8 abs (1024 ulp)
+colmajor 5376x5376  worst 3.576e-7 abs
+colmajor 5376x21504 worst 2.980e-7 abs
+colmajor 21504x5376 worst 1.013e-6 abs
+rowmajor 640x1024   worst 3.353e-8 abs
+rowmajor 1408x1024  worst 6.706e-8 abs
+rowmajor 5376x5376  worst 2.682e-7 abs
+rowmajor 5376x21504 worst 2.980e-7 abs
+rowmajor 21504x5376 worst 9.537e-7 abs
+```
+
+The worst absolute divergence against M=1 is **~1e-6, and it grows with `in_features`** (more
+accumulation steps) — so §3's "5.96e-8 worst" was a small-shape number, not a global one. The
+1e-4 budget has **two** orders of headroom, not four. Corrected in the test file. The ulp column
+is not the thing to read: these dots cancel, so ulp-of-the-result is ~1000x the divergence
+expressed in ulp of the running sum.
+
+**`rows_agree` is true for the row-major kernel even with fast math on**, on all five shapes, unlike
+its column-major twin which needed a pinned association to agree. Not yet explained; recorded
+because it is a difference between the twins that a future edit should not silently change.
+
+Sabotage (AGENTS.md §3): `x + m * K` -> `x + (m == 3 ? 2 : m) * K` in the unrolled loop fails
+`every_row_stays_within_the_fp32_budget_of_the_m1_gemv` with **row 3 only, all five row-major
+shapes**, worst 0.33-2.54 absolute (~4 orders above the budget), col-major untouched. Reverted.
+
+Regression: 19 tests green across `decode_matvec_golden` (2), `repacked_golden` (2),
+`gemm_repack_tile_variants` (3), `live_kernel_bit_exact` (1), `speculative_module` (1),
+`norm_rope_qkv_bit_exact` (4), `rate4_colmajor_bit_exact` (6). `smallm_gemv_bit_exact` 5/5.
+`the_two_word_window_is_bit_identical_to_the_three_word_original` is what licenses the test's
+comparison of `_rm_m4` (written against `_k3`) to `_w2` (the kernel `ishizuki_gemv_pipeline`
+actually selects for `perm_mode != 1`) — same arithmetic, one fewer load.
+
+### 4.5 Status and what is next
+
+The kernel-level claim is verified; the **end-to-end** claim (coherent text, then a tok/s number)
+is **not** — the box was held by another agent's model load for the whole of this session
+(`scripts/gpuq try` -> exit 75, eight consecutive attempts). Do not read T8c as an end-to-end
+result until that run is in the notes.
+
+Order of remaining work, highest leverage first:
+
+1. **End-to-end run of T8c.** `KAPLUMBA_SMALLM_LOG=1 KAPLUMBA_SPEC_TRACE=1 scripts/gpuq try --
+   ./target/release/kaplumba --model ~/.kaplumba/models/Gxma/Gxma --prompt "Explain the theory of
+   relativity." --max-new-tokens 48 --temperature 0 --metrics`. Expect `perm=0` in the log and
+   coherent text. Until this passes, T8c is a kernel result only.
+2. **The drafter forward, ~99 ms x k=3 = ~300 ms/round.** This, not the verify pass, is now the
+   dominant term. `DrafterModel::forward` issues ~28 dispatches per step and **every**
+   `AffineKernels::gemv_buffers` call creates its own command buffer and calls
+   `wait_until_completed()` (`crates/kernels/src/affine.rs:105`), so each of the 22 GEMVs is a full
+   GPU round trip. The 0.28 GB of weights read is ~1 ms of bandwidth; the rest is launch+sync.
+   Every kernel needed to fix it already exists as an `encode_*` buffer API
+   (`elements.rs`: `encode_rms_norm`, `encode_rms_norm_residual`, `encode_rms_norm_residual_layer_scale`,
+   `encode_residual_add`, `encode_layer_scale`, `encode_gated_gelu`, `encode_rope`;
+   `attention.rs`: `encode_online_attention`). The change is: add
+   `AffineKernels::encode_gemv_buffers`, make the drafter's scratch device-resident, and encode the
+   whole forward into **one** command buffer. `tests/drafter_forward.rs` pins the numerical contract
+   against MLX golden arrays, so a refactor can be checked for bit-identity.
+3. Pad the batch to 4 rows. Lower priority than it looked: `main.rs:130` passes `draft_k = 5`, which
+   `SpeculativeEngine::load` clamps to 3 (`speculative.rs:140`), so `k = 3` and the batch is 4 rows
+   for all but the last few tokens of a generation.
+4. `extract_shared_kv` still moves ~268 MB/round to the host at 16k. It only has to be device-resident
+   once item 2 lands, because the drafter's reads are the only consumer.
