+# Plan: Pure Autoregressive Decode $\ge 20\text{ tok/s}$
+
+## 1. Architecture & Technical Design
+
+### 1.1 EXL3 Inner-Loop Integer Latency Hiding
+In `linear_repacked.metal`, the MCG decode loop currently evaluates serialized `IMUL -> AND -> XOR` operations for each tile code. By restructuring the loop to unroll 4 tiles per lane iteration (as verified in `alu_boost.rs` `arm_decode_u4`), instructions across independent tiles interleave, allowing the GPU execution units to sustain high IPC without waiting for dependent latency chains.
+
+### 1.2 Grid Widening for Single-Threadgroup Kernels
+`linear.rs` dispatches `encode_finalize_o_proj_and_pre_ffn_transform_sk2`, `encode_cross_layer_finalize_and_norm`, and `encode_input_norm_and_qkv_transform` with `dispatch_groups(..., 1, 1024)` or `(1, 256)`. Because each SM/core can only execute one threadgroup at a time, a single threadgroup dispatch leaves 19 out of 20 cores idle and incurs a ~4.6 µs scheduling penalty. We widen these kernels to partition work across 42–168 threadgroups (1 wave).
+
+### 1.3 Command Encoder & Pipeline Rolling
+Ensure command buffers maintain sufficient lookahead so that the GPU never drains the queue between layers.
+
+---
+
+## 2. File / Module Touchpoints
+
+- `crates/kernels/src/linear_repacked.metal`: Unroll tile decoding in `extract_v_mcg_32` and production GEMV kernels.
+- `crates/kernels/src/linear.metal` & `crates/kernels/src/linear.rs`: Widen grids of single-threadgroup finalize kernels to multi-threadgroups.
+- `crates/kernels/src/encoder.rs` / `crates/engine/src/model.rs`: Maintain optimal rolling buffer dispatch.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Risks & Fallbacks
+
+- **Risk:** Bit-exact divergence if unrolling alters float accumulation order.
+  - **Mitigation:** Unrolling is strictly applied to the integer dequantization and address generation; float accumulation order is held identical, verified by `live_kernel_bit_exact.rs`.
+- **Risk:** Register spilling if unroll factor is too high.
+  - **Mitigation:** The unroll factor is capped at 4, which `alu_boost.rs` confirmed maintains zero register spills.
